#kaocha/v1
{:tests
 [;; Unit tests — skip ^:integration.
  ;;
  ;; `:kaocha/parallelism` is wired by `:kaocha.plugin/parallel` (see
  ;; `test/kaocha/plugin/parallel.clj`). NSs that redef process-wide
  ;; symbols (`jdbc/execute!`, `registry/rich-type-of`, ...) carry
  ;; `^:serial` in their ns meta — those run sequentially BEFORE the
  ;; parallel pool starts. Everything else runs on a fixed N-thread
  ;; pool.
  {:id :unit
   :test-paths ["test"]
   ;; `:container-e2e` (the two-executor fleet test) needs the locally-built
   ;; `graphden-executor:latest` image + ~4 min of container boots — skip it in
   ;; the fast unit run; it has its own suite below.
   ;;
   ;; `:perf` records SQL-round-trip costs rather than asserting behaviour, and
   ;; it counts into the same process-global counters this run reports — leaving
   ;; it in would both slow the suite and inflate its own numbers.
   :skip-meta [:integration :container-e2e :perf]
   :kaocha/parallelism 8}

  ;; Integration tests — run against the shared PG container. Each NS
  ;; fixture is scoped to its OWN logical DB on the cluster (see
  ;; `graphden.test-infra.shared-container/shared-container-fixture` +
  ;; `ensure-ns-database!`) so that whenever in-JVM parallelism is
  ;; eventually un-blocked, the `DROP SCHEMA public CASCADE` between
  ;; tests can't trample a concurrent NS's tables. Today still
  ;; sequential — see the unit-suite note above for the blocker.
  {:id :integration
   :test-paths ["test"]
   :focus-meta [:integration]
   ;; Per-NS logical DBs (shared-container plugin) keep integration
   ;; tests isolated even when their NSs run on parallel threads.
   ;; N=4 (not 8) for integration: tests that boot their own
   ;; testcontainers (e.g. `clients.vault-test` runs OpenBao) race
   ;; on Docker daemon throughput when many concurrent
   ;; `GenericContainer.start` calls land at once — the `Wait/forHttp`
   ;; ready-check times out before the container actually boots.
   ;; Constraint is on the Docker daemon, not our code.
   :kaocha/parallelism 4}

  ;; Container fleet e2e — boots the locally-built `graphden-executor:latest`
  ;; TWICE as a real two-pod fleet over a shared Postgres (testcontainers) and
  ;; drives the cross-container control plane over real HTTP. Kept OUT of :unit
  ;; and :integration (needs the built image + ~4 min); run explicitly after a
  ;; `bb rebuild`, like the Playwright e2e. `clojure -M:dev:test -m kaocha.runner
  ;; :container-e2e`.
  {:id :container-e2e
   :test-paths ["test"]
   :focus-meta [:container-e2e]}

  ;; Perf scenarios — what each API-level operation COSTS in SQL round trips,
  ;; recorded via `graphden.perf.sql` into perf/runs/perf.edn for `bb perf` to
  ;; gate. Not correctness: the same handlers are asserted by the unit suite.
  ;; Kept out of :unit / :integration so a budget change sends you to `bb perf`
  ;; rather than reading as a broken test, and so the suites stay about
  ;; behaviour. `clojure -M:dev:test -m kaocha.runner :perf`.
  ;; Runs with `--no-randomize`, and that is load-bearing — see the `test-perf`
  ;; task in bb.edn, which owns the flag and explains why. Short version: kaocha
  ;; has NO per-suite randomize toggle (the plugin reads its key from the config
  ;; root only), so there is nothing to put here; a config-shaped answer would
  ;; have to disable randomisation for every suite.
  {:id :perf
   :test-paths ["test"]
   :focus-meta [:perf]}]

 ;; Plugins:
 ;; - profiling: shows slowest tests
 ;; - shared-container: singleton PG container + per-NS logical-DB lifecycle
 ;; - perf: persists what the run cost — the per-NS fixture-vs-assertion split
 ;;   plus the structural counters — to perf/runs/*.edn. It measures nothing
 ;;   itself: it reads profiling's per-testable durations, so it MUST stay
 ;;   listed after profiling.
 :plugins [:kaocha.plugin/profiling
           :kaocha.plugin/parallel
           :kaocha.plugin/shared-container
           :kaocha.plugin/perf]

 ;; Show the slowest 25, not the default 3 — enough of the list to tell one
 ;; outlier from an even tax.
 ;;
 ;; This note used to say "~51 s of fixture against ~0.14 s of assertions" and
 ;; ask whether that 360:1 held across the suite. `kaocha.plugin/perf` now
 ;; answers it on every run, and the answer is: no, and it matters which suite
 ;; you ask about.
 ;;
 ;;   integration   4841 s fixture vs  966 s assertions across 71 NSes    ~5:1
 ;;   unit           114 s fixture vs  520 s assertions across 198 NSes   ~1:4.6
 ;;
 ;; (integration: 2026-08-07 gate run. unit: 2026-08-22, 1777 deftests, 160 s
 ;; wall at parallelism 8. Earlier unit readings: 341/607/165, and before the
 ;; fixture diet 3210/335/69.) So the old conclusion — the fixture is the
 ;; target, per-test tuning is noise — holds for INTEGRATION and is wrong for
 ;; UNIT, which is not fixture-dominated at all and has got less so.
 ;;
 ;; Read the big integration fixture numbers carefully: they are largely
 ;; LOCK-WAIT on the two shared per-package-set warm-up chains (golden
 ;; bootstrap → cached sweep → first compile, once per JVM × set), not
 ;; per-NS work. See memory `project_test_fixture_economics` and
 ;; test-infra.shared-bootstrap.
 ;;
 ;; WHERE THE UNIT SUITE'S WALL-CLOCK ACTUALLY IS (2026-08-22 audit). At
 ;; parallelism 8, 160 s of wall over 520 s of assertions means the run is
 ;; bounded by its longest single namespace, and two of them are most of it:
 ;;
 ;;   packages.registry-test   91 s   (24 tests; each install/fork/materialise
 ;;                                    recompiles the graph — that IS the subject)
 ;;   packages.export-test     77 s
 ;;   everything else         <=31 s
 ;;
 ;; So: work aimed at making this suite faster only pays inside those two.
 ;; The tempting target — the handful of namespaces whose fixture dwarfs their
 ;; assertions (7.4 s of setup for 0.5 s of checks, and five like it) — is a
 ;; measurement artifact of the design working: that cost is the per-NS logical
 ;; DB clone that buys parallel isolation, they all share the sanctioned
 ;; `[core web app]` golden, and removing every one of them would not move the
 ;; wall by a second because they run inside registry-test's shadow. Check a
 ;; namespace's `packages` vector before accepting its cost; do not chase the
 ;; clone.
 ;;
 ;; Numbers here are per-thread and overlap under `:kaocha/parallelism`; they
 ;; rank namespaces, they do not sum to wall-clock. See docs/PERF_BUDGETS.md.
 :kaocha.plugin.profiling/count 25}
